<section id="stories">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="section-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0b4228" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9l5 5v10a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2z"/><path d="M15 4v5h5"/><path d="M8 12h8 M8 16h5"/></svg></div>
      <h2>Five women, five kinds of alone</h2>
      <p>These are composite accounts, written to reflect struggles many women live through quietly. No names are used. If you'd like your own story featured, real or anonymous, see the bottom of this page.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="story">
      <div class="story-meta">
        <div class="motif"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0b4228" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 3 L12 14 M12 3 C9 3 7 5 7 8 L17 8 C17 5 15 3 12 3 M6 21 L18 21 M9 21 L7 14 M15 21 L17 14"/></svg></div>
        Domestic labor
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>She cleans other people's houses. No one is cleaning up after hers.</h3>
        <p>She's in her thirties, works as a housemaid, and is raising two children mostly on her own — her husband brings in little and less each year. Her days are entirely accounted for: someone else's home in the morning, her own children at night, and nothing left over for herself. There's no one asking whether the arrangement is fair to her, whether the money adds up, or what happens if she gets sick for a week. She isn't looking for pity. She's looking for someone to help her get what she and her children are actually owed — support from her husband, and a way to stand on her own if he won't provide it.</p>
        <p class="byline">— a composite account, shared to represent a common struggle</p>
        <div class="encourage-line"><strong>What she deserves to hear:</strong> that showing up every single day for two children, on wages that don't reflect the work, is not invisible. It's the reason her household runs at all.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="story">
      <div class="story-meta">
        <div class="motif"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#a53860" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 21s-7-4.6-9.3-9C1.2 8.6 3 5.5 6.2 5.2c2-.2 3.4 1 4.3 2.3.6-1 2.2-2.5 4.3-2.3 3.2.3 5 3.4 3.5 6.8C19 16.4 12 21 12 21z"/></svg></div>
        Immigration & family
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>A nurse, a mother, and a marriage that couldn't survive a visa</h3>
        <p>She's a nurse with a young child. Her husband left — not over love, but over a visa he couldn't get and a living he felt he had to chase elsewhere. She's now raising their child while working shifts most people wouldn't choose, in a country that isn't fully hers to stay in either. Her struggle isn't dramatic; it's administrative, exhausting, and lonely. It's paperwork and waiting rooms and a child who needs stability she's not sure she can promise. She needs someone who understands both immigration systems and single-parent survival — because right now she's navigating both alone.</p>
        <p class="byline">— a composite account, shared to represent a common struggle</p>
        <div class="encourage-line"><strong>What she deserves to hear:</strong> that she's held a household, a career, and a child's sense of stability together with no partner splitting the load — and that takes a kind of strength that doesn't get a certificate.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="story">
      <div class="story-meta">
        <div class="motif"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#c98a2c" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3c-1.8 2-2.5 4-2.5 6a2.5 2.5 0 0 0 5 0c0-2-.7-4-2.5-6z M6 14c1.5-1 3.5-1.5 6-1.5s4.5.5 6 1.5c0 3.5-2.7 6-6 6.5C8.7 20 6 17.5 6 14z"/></svg></div>
        Widowhood & inheritance
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>She raised her children before she'd finished growing up herself. Then she was widowed.</h3>
        <p>She was married young, before she'd had much say in the matter, and spent her twenties raising children rather than building anything of her own — no career, no savings in her name, no independent footing. Then her husband died, and the life she'd built around him didn't come with a safety net for her. What she was left with wasn't security; it was the same responsibilities, minus the person who used to share them, and often minus any legal or financial claim to what they'd built together. Her question isn't rhetorical: after everything she gave, what does she actually have a right to now?</p>
        <p class="byline">— a composite account, shared to represent a common struggle</p>
        <div class="encourage-line"><strong>What she deserves to hear:</strong> that she raised her children well before she'd finished raising herself, and that her worth was never supposed to depend on someone else being alive to vouch for it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="story">
      <div class="story-meta">
        <div class="motif"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0b4228" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3c1 0 2 .8 2 2s-1 2-2 2-2-.8-2-2 1-2 2-2z M9 8h6l-1 5-1 8h-1l-.5-6-1 6h-1l-1-8-1-5z"/></svg></div>
        Widowhood & disability
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Her legs are held together with rods. She still shows up for her mother.</h3>
        <p>She was widowed, and not long after, an injury left her with rods in both legs — walking is slow now, and some days it's simply painful. None of that has stopped her. She still makes her way to her mother's flat, still runs the errands, still does what needs doing, because there's no one else lined up to do it if she doesn't. Nobody is asking what her recovery actually requires, or what happens to her mother's care on the days her legs won't cooperate. She's not asking to be excused from responsibility — she's asking for it to be shared, and for care that accounts for her own body, not just everyone else's needs.</p>
        <p class="byline">— a composite account, shared to represent a common struggle</p>
        <div class="encourage-line"><strong>What she deserves to hear:</strong> that walking to her mother's flat on legs held together with rods is not a small thing she should quietly keep doing unnoticed. It's devotion, and someone should say so out loud.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="story">
      <div class="story-meta">
        <div class="motif"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#a53860" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M6 4h9l3 3v13H6z M15 4v3h3 M9 12h6 M9 15h6 M9 18h4"/></svg></div>
        Domestic labor & education
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>She wrote down every hard day. Then she spent it on her children's school fees.</h3>
        <p>She works as a housemaid, and for years she kept a record — not for anyone else, just for herself — of what the work actually cost her: the wages that never quite stretched, the days she couldn't afford to be sick, the small humiliations most people never see. She didn't write it down to complain. She wrote it down so she wouldn't forget what she was working toward. Every rupee she could spare went toward getting her children into a good school, the kind she never had access to herself. She's still doing the same hard work today. The difference is her children won't have to.</p>
        <p class="byline">— a composite account, shared to represent a common struggle</p>
        <div class="encourage-line"><strong>What she deserves to hear:</strong> that turning her own hardship into her children's education, deliberately and on purpose, is one of the most quietly heroic things a person can do — and it should never go unsaid.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="story-note">
      <strong>A note on these stories:</strong> the accounts above are composites written to represent struggles many women face, not verified individual testimonies about a specific identifiable person. If this page grows into a space for real, first-person submissions, each one should be reviewed with the contributor's informed consent before publishing — including how identifying details are handled and whether names are used.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="encouragement">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div class="section-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#a53860" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 20.5s-7.5-4.6-9.7-9.4C1 7.8 3 4.6 6.4 4.3c2.1-.2 3.6 1 4.6 2.4.7-1.1 2.4-2.6 4.6-2.4 3.4.3 5.4 3.5 3.7 6.8C19.5 15.9 12 20.5 12 20.5z"/></svg></div>
      <h2>Words for her</h2>
      <p>Appreciation these women rarely hear said out loud. Read what others have left, or add your own.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="notes-grid">
      <div class="note-card">
        "You are not invisible. Someone, somewhere, sees exactly how much you're carrying — and it does not go unnoticed by everyone."
        <div class="who">— a reader</div>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        "The fact that you keep showing up, even on the days it costs you something, is its own kind of strength. That deserves to be said to your face."
        <div class="who">— a reader</div>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        "You didn't just survive what happened to you — you kept other people standing too. That's not small. That's everything."
        <div class="who">— a reader</div>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        "Whoever is reading this and doing more than anyone acknowledges: I see you, and I'm proud of you."
        <div class="who">— a reader</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <form class="encourage-form" action="https://formspree.io/f/mjybnker" method="POST">
      <input type="hidden" name="_subject" value="New encouragement note — Heard, But Unheard">
      <p style="margin:0 0 14px;font-size:15px;color:var(--ink-soft);">Leave your own words of encouragement below — it may be added to this wall.</p>
      <div class="form-row">
        <input type="text" name="name" placeholder="Your name (optional)">
      </div>
      <div class="form-row">
        <textarea name="message" placeholder="Write a few words of encouragement..." rows="3" required></textarea>
      </div>
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn-solid" style="border-color:var(--rani);background:var(--rani);">Send encouragement</button>
    </form>
  </div>
</section>

